bb77eaa53b"] [17592186184323 :knowledge-point/questions "question-5eff477c-d77b-5edc-9e57-fb4474642e96"] ["question-d53c2052-a67a-5988-8f1a-4c9a720c7a75" :question/id #uuid "d53c2052-a67a-5988-8f1a-4c9a720c7a75"] ["question-d53c2052-a67a-5988-8f1a-4c9a720c7a75" :question/math-academy-id "q-52532"] ["question-d53c2052-a67a-5988-8f1a-4c9a720c7a75" :question/problem "Rewrite $-4{x}^{2}+8x$ by completing the square."] ["question-d53c2052-a67a-5988-8f1a-4c9a720c7a75" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-4.$ So, we take out a factor of $-4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-4({x}^{2}-2x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-4({(x-1)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+(-4)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-d53c2052-a67a-5988-8f1a-4c9a720c7a75" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-167b595a-7f5f-59ff-991b-3067a90e82fd" :answer-field/id #uuid "167b595a-7f5f-59ff-991b-3067a90e82fd"] ["answer-3b842baa-f6f2-52b1-8460-09b16f0bcd80" :answer/id #uuid "3b842baa-f6f2-52b1-8460-09b16f0bcd80"] ["answer-3b842baa-f6f2-52b1-8460-09b16f0bcd80" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3b842baa-f6f2-52b1-8460-09b16f0bcd80" :answer/value "-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4"] ["answer-b561b8c7-15f6-5cfb-b205-0138ec744c67" :answer/id #uuid "b561b8c7-15f6-5cfb-b205-0138ec744c67"] ["answer-b561b8c7-15f6-5cfb-b205-0138ec744c67" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answ